ogles2.library 1.4 beta 1 (release 1.4-beta1) for AmigaOS 3.x on PiStorm
=========================================================================

OpenGL ES 2.0 for 68k AmigaOS, drawn by the Raspberry Pi's GPU through
PiStorm and Emu68: the Raspberry Pi 4's (Broadcom V3D 4.2) and, new in
1.4, the Raspberry Pi 3's (VideoCore IV). One library and one shader
compiler for both; the library finds which Pi it runs on. The API is
source-compatible with AmigaOS 4's ogles2.library: the GLES2 functions plus
the agl* context calls.

This is a beta
--------------
1.4 beta 1 is the first release with the Raspberry Pi 3. On the Pi 4 it
behaves as release 1.3.3 (the same tests, pictures and speed); what is new
there is only the shader cache's size limit (see "Shaders"). Please report
what you find, with the log (see "Logging").

Requirements
------------
- An Amiga with a PiStorm and a Raspberry Pi 4B or CM4, or a Raspberry Pi
  3B or 3B+ (the GPU is the Pi's). Tested: a Pi 4B and a Pi 3B on a
  PiStorm32-lite in an A1200.
- Emu68 (tested: 1.1.0-beta.1)
- AmigaOS 3.x (tested: 3.2) with Picasso96 or CyberGraphX
- A 32-bit RTG screen for programs that render into a window

The Raspberry Pi 3
------------------
The Pi 3's GPU is older and its Emu68 slower: Quake III's timedemo runs at
about 33 fps (89 on a Pi 4 at 2.2 GHz), glmark2 scores 70. Textures and
framebuffers go up to 2048 x 2048 (4096 on the Pi 4), with 8 vertex
attributes (16). Blending, texture formats and vertex formats are done in
the shaders on this GPU, so a program needs a shader "variant" for each
combination of them it draws with: the compiler makes them as the program
draws (each new combination once, then kept in OGLES2Cache: as a .vc4k
file), or the program ships them (see "Shaders"). Programs and their
binaries are the same source for both Pis; the binaries are not: a .v3dp
file is the Pi 4's, a .vc4p file (with its .vc4k) the Pi 3's.

What is in this archive
-----------------------
  Install-ogles2      the installer (an AmigaDOS script, see below)
  Libs/ogles2.library the library
  Libs/v3dglslc       the shader compiler (Mesa's V3D compiler, 19 MB)
  C/OGLES2Log         writes the library's log while a program runs
  Examples/           four programs with their compiled shaders
  SDK/                headers, import libraries, documentation and the
                      examples' sources, for programmers (only in
                      ogles2-1.4-beta1-sdk.lha; see "For programmers")
  LICENSE, licences/  licences and notices

Installing
----------
From a Shell, in this drawer:

    Execute Install-ogles2

It asks before each step:
- copies ogles2.library to LIBS:;
- copies the shader compiler v3dglslc to LIBS: (optional, see "Shaders");
- makes the drawer SYS:OGLES2Cache and adds "Assign OGLES2Cache:
  SYS:OGLES2Cache" to S:User-Startup, so compiled shaders are kept across
  reboots (optional; without it they are kept in T: until the next reboot);
- copies OGLES2Log to C: (optional).
"Execute Install-ogles2 ALL" does all four without asking.

To remove it: delete LIBS:ogles2.library, LIBS:v3dglslc, C:OGLES2Log and
SYS:OGLES2Cache, and the lines between ";BEGIN ogles2" and ";END ogles2"
in S:User-Startup.

Using it without installing
---------------------------
A program finds ogles2.library and the compiler in its own drawer when they
are not installed: the library in LIBS:, else PROGDIR:libs/, else beside the
program; the compiler likewise (LIBS:v3dglslc, PROGDIR:libs/v3dglslc,
PROGDIR:v3dglslc). So a program can be shipped with a libs/ drawer holding
ogles2.library (and v3dglslc, if it needs to compile shaders). This works
for programs built with this SDK's import library (version 1.3 or newer).
A program's own copy also replaces an installed one that is too old for
it, unless another program is using the old one at that moment (AmigaOS
keeps one ogles2.library in memory for all programs).

To try the examples this way: copy Libs/ogles2.library into Examples/libs/
and run Examples/triangle, Examples/cube or Examples/boingocean.

Shaders
-------
The GPU runs machine code compiled from each program's GLSL. ogles2 finds
a program's compiled form ("binary", a file named by a hash of the GLSL
text: .v3dp on the Pi 4, .vc4p on the Pi 3) in the program's shaders/
drawer, beside the program, or in OGLES2Cache: (or T:). When there is
none, the compiler v3dglslc compiles it (about 0.2 s per program; its first
start takes about a second) and the result goes into OGLES2Cache:, so the
next run needs no compiler.

The cache does not grow without end: over 64 MB or 8192 files, the first
compile after ogles2.library is loaded removes the oldest of its files, down
to 3/4 of the limit, and a program that needs one again compiles it again.
"SetEnv OGLES2/CacheKB 200000" (in S:User-Startup, to keep it) sets the size
in KB; 0 turns this off. Keep the binaries you ship in the program's shaders/
drawer, which is never touched.

Programs that ship their binaries (as the examples do) run without the
compiler. Where it is installed, they leave it unstarted only if they also
ship their shaders' records (.v3ds files, in shaders/ with the binaries),
as the examples do. Programs that make shaders at run time (gl4es
and the programs built on it, for instance) need it.

On the Pi 3 a program without the compiler also needs the variants of every
state it draws in (see "The Raspberry Pi 3"): in its .vc4p, or in a .vc4k
beside it. The examples ship theirs for a 32-bit RTG screen as PiStorm's
VideoCore RTG driver gives it. A program that finds no variant for a draw
and no compiler draws with the closest one it has, which can show wrong
colours: install the compiler on a Pi 3 unless a program says it ships
everything it needs.

The examples
------------
  Examples/triangle   a turning coloured triangle in a window
  Examples/cube       a textured cube with a depth buffer, in a window you
                      can resize
  Examples/boingocean the Boing ball floating on a ray-marched ocean, all
                      drawn by shaders: 60 fps at 640 x 480 on a Pi 4 with
                      "boingocean FULLSCREEN" from a Shell (about 55 in a
                      window); drag with the mouse to move the camera, keys
                      1 to 4 draw it at 1/1 to 1/4 size and scale it up
  Examples/boing2026  the 1984 Boing Ball remade: it bounces in a neon room
                      with a soft shadow, a reflecting floor, bloom, 4x
                      antialiasing and its "boing" on Paula (about 53 fps
                      at 640 x 480); "boing2026 TOUR" shows each effect in
                      turn. Its keys and options: Examples/boing2026.txt
Close the window or press Esc to quit. They have icons: from Workbench
their options are the icons' ToolTypes (switched off in brackets, as
"(FULLSCREEN)"). Their binaries are in
Examples/shaders/ (.v3dp for the Pi 4, .vc4p for the Pi 3): they run
without the compiler on either Pi. The frame rates above are the Pi 4's.

Logging
-------
The library logs to T:ogles2.log, or to the file named in the environment
variable OGLES2/LogFile (SetEnv OGLES2/LogFile RAM:ogles2.log). OGLES2Log
writes the buffered log while a program still runs. A program that fails
usually says why there.

For programmers
---------------
The SDK comes in two forms: ogles2-1.4-beta1-sdk.lha (this archive with
SDK/ added) and ogles2-1.4-beta1-sdk.zip (the SDK alone, for building on a
PC with a cross compiler). SDL2 and SDL3 with an ogles2 backend are a
separate archive, ogles2-1.4-beta1-sdl.lha or .zip, which unpacks over the
SDK (its ReadMe-SDL). It holds:

SDK/include    <proto/ogles2.h> (GLES2 and agl*), <libraries/ogles2.h>
               (tags, errors, extra enumerants), the dispatch table, and
               Khronos' GLES2/ and KHR/ headers
SDK/lib        libogles2.a (GCC) and ogles2.lib (vbcc), the import library
SDK/doc        GettingStarted.txt: writing, building, debugging and shipping
               a program, and a prompt for working with an AI assistant;
               ogles2.doc: the agl* calls, shaders and binaries, extensions
               and deviations from GLES2 and from AmigaOS 4's library
SDK/examples   the examples' sources and a Makefile (GCC and vbcc)

The import library and the headers are MIT-licensed: programs of any
licence may use them. The source of everything here, the compiler's
included, is at https://github.com/ladislawv/ogles2pi3 (the 1.4 betas; the
releases before them: https://github.com/ladislawv/ogles2).

Conformance
-----------
The Khronos dEQP-GLES2 must-pass list (17,165 cases), run on an A1200 with
this release's library:
- Pi 4: all pass except 25 cube-map cases (the GPU always filters cube maps
  seamlessly, as GLES 3 requires) and 6 timing and quality warnings.
- Pi 3: 17,047 pass; 18 fail, each also failing with Mesa's own VideoCore
  IV driver on Linux on the same Pi (which passes 16,906); 92 are not
  supported (sizes and formats the GPU lacks).
The SDK's ogles2.doc lists the deviations.

Licence
-------
ogles2.library incorporates PiStorm3D's V3D backend and is distributed
under the Hyperion MiniGL Open Source License (licences/
License_MiniGL_Library.txt): for AmigaOS only, with the source published.
The import library, headers, examples and OGLES2Log are MIT. v3dglslc is
Mesa (MIT; licences/Mesa/). LICENSE lists every part.

